{
  "predicted_winner": "Respondent",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.35,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to treat the “arm of the State” inquiry as a federal-law question that gives substantial (often decisive) weight to whether the State is legally or practically on the hook for the entity’s liabilities, consistent with the Court’s sovereign-immunity cases emphasizing protection of the state treasury and state dignity. Given NJ Transit’s close statutory ties to New Jersey and the State’s asserted fiscal responsibility/oversight, the Court will probably conclude NJ Transit qualifies as an arm of the State for purposes of interstate sovereign immunity. The Court is also likely to reject any waiver theory absent an unmistakably clear statement that New Jersey consented to private suits against NJ Transit in other States’ courts. A dissent would likely argue for a more historically grounded, corporation-sensitive approach and a multi-factor test that does not let treasury exposure dominate."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.62,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to read MPPAA’s withdrawal-liability scheme as fixing the relevant actuarial assumptions to those in effect for the plan year ending before withdrawal (or at least as of the initial assessment), because the statute’s timing/notice/arbitration framework presupposes a determinate liability that can be promptly demanded and contested. Allowing retroactive interest-rate changes would create open-ended reassessment risk, undermining predictability for collective bargaining and inviting strategic manipulation—concerns the Court often treats as strong indicators against implied retroactivity absent clear authorization. The Court can accommodate the Fund’s accuracy/solvency concerns by holding that plans may change assumptions prospectively (and correct clerical errors), but may not retroactively reprice past-year liabilities to increase a withdrawing employer’s bill. Given the existing circuit authority (e.g., Metz) and the absence of an express statutory mechanism authorizing retroactive assumption revisions akin to the provisions governing plan amendments, the Petitioners have the edge."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Respondent",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.25,
  "predicted_split": "7-2",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to hold that RLUIPA’s “appropriate relief against a government” does not clearly authorize individual-capacity money damages, especially given RLUIPA’s Spending Clause foundation and the Court’s insistence on unmistakably clear notice before attaching funding conditions that expose state actors to monetary liability. Sossamon’s treatment of the same remedial phrase as open-ended and insufficiently clear in an immunity-sensitive context will probably carry substantial weight, and the Court may distinguish Tanzin on the ground that RFRA is not Spending Clause legislation and applies to federal officials. The Court will likely conclude that equitable relief remains available and that expanding RLUIPA to personal damages is a policy choice for Congress to state expressly. A couple of Justices may dissent based on Tanzin’s historical understanding of “appropriate relief” and the practical inadequacy of injunction-only remedies for completed or mooted prison violations."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Respondent",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.35,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to extend its structural view of interstate sovereign immunity (Hyatt III) to state instrumentalities and to treat NJ Transit as an arm of New Jersey. Consistent with Regents v. Doe and Hess, the Justices will probably give substantial—often effectively decisive—weight to whether state law makes the State legally responsible for judgments or otherwise exposes the state fisc, while also crediting New Jersey’s governance and oversight features. The Court is also likely to reject any waiver theory because consent to suit in another State’s courts must be clear and express, and the New Jersey Tort Claims Act does not unmistakably provide that. Even if NJ Transit has operational and commercial characteristics, the Court’s current federalism/dignity jurisprudence tends to resolve close arm-of-the-state questions in favor of immunity."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Respondent",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.35,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to read ERISA/MPPAA’s actuarial-assumptions provisions (especially §1393’s “best estimate” and the arbitration presumptions of correctness) as giving substantial discretion to plan actuaries to use reasonable assumptions for the measurement-date valuation, even if finalized shortly after the close of the plan year. It will probably characterize the Fund’s action as a permissible refinement/correction of the assumptions used to value unfunded vested benefits as of the measurement date, rather than an impermissible retroactive “do-over” after withdrawal. The Court’s recent tendency to defer to statutory text and administrability, plus concern about underfunding and cost-shifting to remaining employers/participants, favors allowing such recalculations subject to the statute’s reasonableness and error-review standards in arbitration. A dissent would stress finality, notice-and-demand timing, and the employer’s reliance interests reflected in decisions like Metz."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Respondent",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.25,
  "predicted_split": "7-2",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to hold that RLUIPA’s authorization of “appropriate relief against a government” does not clearly provide for individual-capacity money damages, especially given RLUIPA’s Spending Clause basis and the Court’s insistence on clear notice of funding conditions and remedies. Sossamon’s narrow reading of the same remedial phrase in the sovereign-immunity context will likely carry over, with the Court distinguishing Tanzin on the ground that RFRA is not Spending Clause legislation and applies to federal officials directly. The Court will probably conclude that, at most, RLUIPA permits injunctive and declaratory relief against state officials (and perhaps official-capacity relief where not barred), but not damages against them personally absent unmistakable statutory language. A small dissent would likely argue that Tanzin’s textual reading of “appropriate relief” and RLUIPA’s broader remedial structure support damages to prevent mootness and ensure meaningful enforcement."
}

{
  "focal_point": "Whether, and under what circumstances, Sections 32(1)(a) and 43(a)(1)(A) of the Lanham Act allow a plaintiff to obtain liability and remedies for trademark “use in commerce” that occurred outside the United States (i.e., the Act’s extraterritorial reach).",
  "legal_provision": "Lanham Act, 15 U.S.C. § 1114(1)(a) (Section 32(1)(a), infringement of registered marks) and 15 U.S.C. § 1125(a)(1)(A) (Section 43(a)(1)(A), false designation of origin/unregistered mark infringement), interpreted through the Supreme Court’s presumption against extraterritoriality framework.",
  "petitioner_position": "Petitioners (Abitron) argue that the strong presumption against extraterritoriality controls and is not rebutted by the Lanham Act’s text; trademarks are historically territorial, so U.S. trademark claims should not reach foreign companies’ foreign sales in foreign markets. They contend the Tenth Circuit’s approach improperly allowed Lanham Act liability and damages based on predominantly foreign conduct, effectively exporting U.S. trademark law and creating international friction.",
  "respondent_position": "Respondent (Hetronic) maintains that the Lanham Act can apply when foreign trademark use causes sufficient U.S.-connected harm—particularly confusion or effects in U.S. commerce—and that limiting the Act strictly to domestic transactions would under-protect U.S. trademark owners against cross-border schemes that divert sales, damage goodwill, or create confusion affecting the United States. Respondent supports an effects-based or U.S.-commerce-focused test that captures foreign conduct with meaningful domestic impact.",
  "key_precedents": [
    "RJR Nabisco, Inc. v. European Community, 579 U.S. 325 (2016)"
  ],
  "legal_complexity": "Complex"
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Respondent",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.25,
  "predicted_split": "7-2",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to hold that § 3730(c)(2)(A) authorizes the United States to move to dismiss a qui tam action even after initially declining to intervene, because the provision’s text imposes only notice and a hearing and does not make intervention a prerequisite. Consistent with the Executive’s Article II enforcement authority and the United States being the real party in interest, the Court will probably adopt a highly deferential standard of review, treating dismissal as presumptively proper absent a clear showing of illegality (e.g., fraud on the court or unconstitutional motive). The Court may characterize the hearing requirement as ensuring the relator can be heard, not as inviting searching merits review of the government’s enforcement calculus. Any stricter “rational relationship” test is likely to be rejected as insufficiently deferential and as raising separation-of-powers concerns."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.62,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "Under the Court’s modern clear-statement framework, filing deadlines are treated as nonjurisdictional unless Congress clearly ties the time bar to the court’s adjudicatory power, and § 2409a(g) uses ordinary limitations language without linking it to the jurisdictional grant in § 1346(f). The Court has repeatedly rejected the idea that a time bar becomes jurisdictional merely because it appears in a sovereign-immunity waiver, emphasizing that such limits are usually claim-processing rules absent a clear statement. Although Block and Mottaz contain language lower courts have read as jurisdictional, the Court is likely to characterize that as pre-clear-statement-era imprecision and harmonize those cases without treating the deadline as jurisdictional. Historical lower-court treatment will matter but likely will not overcome the statute’s text/structure and the Court’s strong recent trend against “drive-by jurisdictional rulings.”"
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.72,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "Applying the Court’s modern presumption-against-extraterritoriality framework (Morrison/RJR Nabisco), Sections 32(1)(a) and 43(a)(1)(A) are best read to have a domestic focus on infringing “use in commerce,” not on downstream foreign effects alone. The Court is likely to reject the Tenth Circuit’s broad effects-based approach and hold that the Lanham Act does not authorize liability and damages for purely foreign sales to foreign consumers absent a domestic use (or at least a use that is sufficiently tied to U.S. commerce, such as importation or domestic sales). That would require vacating the worldwide damages award and limiting relief to the subset of conduct involving U.S. commerce. Several Justices will likely concur/dissent to preserve some room for foreign conduct that causes U.S. confusion, but the majority will emphasize administrable territorial limits to avoid international friction."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.78,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to reject the Second Circuit’s open-ended “reliance-and-control” theory as too vague and prone to criminalizing ordinary political influence, consistent with its recent narrowing of federal corruption and honest-services doctrines to avoid indeterminate standards. It will probably hold that a private citizen owes the public an honest-services fiduciary duty only when he has a clear, formalized duty to the government (e.g., as an actual official/employee/agent or someone formally selected/authorized to act for the government), not merely because officials listen to him. On that approach, Percoco’s conviction would be vacated because the jury instruction allowed conviction without requiring a sufficiently definite source of fiduciary duty. The Court would likely leave room for liability where a private person is formally empowered to act on the government’s behalf, but not under Margiotta-style de facto influence alone."
}

{
  "focal_point": "Whether, and under what test, Sections 32(1)(a) and 43(a)(1)(A) of the Lanham Act allow a U.S. trademark plaintiff to obtain liability and damages for a foreign defendant’s trademark “use in commerce” occurring outside the United States (e.g., foreign sales), consistent with the presumption against extraterritoriality.",
  "legal_provision": "Lanham Act § 32(1)(a), 15 U.S.C. § 1114(1)(a) (infringement of registered marks) and Lanham Act § 43(a)(1)(A), 15 U.S.C. § 1125(a)(1)(A) (false designation of origin/unfair competition), as constrained by the Supreme Court’s presumption against extraterritoriality framework.",
  "petitioner_position": "Petitioners (Abitron) argue that the Lanham Act does not apply extraterritorially to foreign companies’ sales in foreign countries because Congress did not “affirmatively and unmistakably” indicate extraterritorial reach; trademarks are traditionally territorial; and allowing U.S. trademark claims for foreign-market sales would improperly regulate foreign commerce and create international friction. They invoke the modern two-step presumption-against-extraterritoriality analysis (as articulated in RJR Nabisco) to contend that the relevant provisions’ focus is not foreign conduct and thus foreign sales/uses fall outside the statute.",
  "respondent_position": "Respondent (Hetronic) argues that the Lanham Act can reach certain foreign trademark uses when they sufficiently affect U.S. commerce and/or cause U.S.-relevant confusion or harm, and that limiting the Act strictly to domestic transactions would under-protect U.S. trademark rights against cross-border infringement schemes. Respondent generally defends the Tenth Circuit’s approach (and similar circuit tests) that permit liability for foreign conduct with substantial effects in the United States, and resists a categorical rule that would immunize foreign sales even when they are closely tied to U.S. commerce or U.S. consumer confusion.",
  "key_precedents": [
    "RJR Nabisco, Inc. v. European Community, 579 U.S. 325 (2016)"
  ],
  "legal_complexity": "Complex"
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.62,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to extend Allina’s textual approach and hold that § 1395hh(a)(2) can require notice-and-comment for CMS guidance that effectively establishes or changes a binding standard governing Medicare payment or eligibility, even if the guidance would be “interpretive” under the APA. A workable standard the Court could adopt is whether the policy has practical legal effect on payment/eligibility (i.e., is treated as controlling in audits/appeals) rather than merely announcing nonbinding enforcement priorities. On that view, time-based inpatient benchmarks in manuals/web postings that function as reimbursement determinants would qualify as “substantive legal standards,” making them invalid absent notice-and-comment. The Court would likely also signal that FCA liability cannot be premised on noncompliance with such unlawfully issued standards, at least where the alleged falsity depends on treating the guidance as legally mandatory."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.68,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "Under the Court’s modern clear-statement rule, filing deadlines are treated as nonjurisdictional unless Congress clearly ties the time bar to the court’s adjudicatory power, and § 2409a(g) uses ordinary limitations language without jurisdictional terms. The QTA’s jurisdictional grant appears separately in 28 U.S.C. § 1346(f), a structural cue the Court has repeatedly relied on (e.g., Wong, Fort Bend, Boechler) to reject jurisdictional characterizations. The government’s sovereign-immunity framing is unlikely to prevail because the Court has said that a time bar’s role in a waiver of immunity does not by itself make it jurisdictional. Prior QTA cases (e.g., Block, Mottaz, Beggerly) are likely read as addressing timeliness/waiver scope without squarely deciding the jurisdictional label, allowing the Court to reclassify the deadline without overruling them."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.72,
  "predicted_split": "9-0",
  "reasoning": "Applying the Court’s modern two-step presumption-against-extraterritoriality framework (RJR Nabisco), the Lanham Act’s infringement provisions lack a clear indication of extraterritorial reach, so liability must rest on a domestic application of the statute. The Court is likely to hold that the relevant statutory “focus” is the defendant’s “use in commerce” of the mark, and that purely foreign sales/uses are not actionable even if they have downstream economic effects on a U.S. trademark owner. It will reject the Tenth Circuit’s broad “substantial effects/diversion of foreign sales” approach as untethered to statutory text and as creating international friction. The Court will likely remand for consideration of which specific transactions involved domestic “use in commerce” (e.g., sales into the United States) and limit damages accordingly."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.72,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to reject the Second Circuit’s open-ended “reliance-and-control” theory as too indeterminate for a federal criminal statute, echoing its recent pattern of narrowing federal corruption theories to avoid vagueness and overbreadth. It will probably hold that honest-services liability for a non-officeholder requires a clear, preexisting fiduciary/agency-type duty grounded in law (e.g., formal government position, formal selection/appointment, or a defined agency relationship), not merely informal influence or access. Because the jury instructions allowed conviction based on an amorphous de facto influence standard, the Court would vacate and remand (or reverse) without foreclosing prosecution under a narrower, better-defined theory. Several Justices will emphasize federalism and First Amendment concerns about chilling ordinary lobbying and political advocacy if “honest services” turns on informal political clout."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Respondent",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.35,
  "predicted_split": "5-4",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to hold that, under the existing Gingles framework and Section 2 precedent, Alabama’s map probably dilutes Black voting strength because Black voters are sufficiently numerous and geographically compact to form a second opportunity district and racially polarized voting would otherwise defeat their candidates of choice. The Court is also likely to accept an “any-part Black” measure as a permissible (and often more faithful) way to identify the minority community for Section 2 purposes, rather than requiring a rigid “single-race Black” metric. On the stay/Preliminary-injunction posture, the Court may treat Purcell as less controlling where the litigation began early and the lower court made detailed factual findings after a full evidentiary hearing. Given the Court’s current composition, the most plausible outcome is a narrow affirmance with a closely divided vote."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.93,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to hold that Harvard’s race-conscious admissions program violates Title VI as interpreted in lockstep with Equal Protection strict scrutiny, concluding that the use of race is not sufficiently measurable, limited, or time-bounded to satisfy narrow tailoring and risks functioning as racial balancing. The current majority has shown strong skepticism toward race-based classifications and is inclined to read Title VI’s nondiscrimination command to forbid intentional race-based decisionmaking absent an exceptionally tight fit. On standing, the Court is likely to find SFFA had associational standing at filing and that the case was not mooted by changes in particular members’ circumstances, avoiding a broad repudiation of associational standing. The result would be reversal for SFFA on the merits, with the standing objections rejected or treated as insufficient to defeat jurisdiction."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Respondent",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.25,
  "predicted_split": "5-4",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to affirm that Alabama’s map probably violates Section 2 under the traditional Gingles framework because the record supports a second reasonably configured Black-opportunity district and shows strong racial polarization and white bloc voting. Recent Court decisions have narrowed some voting-rights doctrines, but they have not overruled Gingles vote-dilution claims, and the district court’s fact-bound findings and illustrative maps make this a difficult case for Alabama to reverse outright. On the demographic-metric issue, the Court is likely to accept an inclusive measure of the Black population (e.g., any-part Black) as permissible for Gingles I because excluding multiracial Black-identifying voters lacks a clear statutory basis and conflicts with how the VRA has been applied in practice. The most plausible outcome is a narrow, closely divided decision leaving Section 2 vote-dilution doctrine largely intact while rejecting Alabama’s attempt to tighten the numerosity/compactness inquiry via a single-race-only metric."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.62,
  "predicted_split": "5-4",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to treat corporate registration plus appointment of an in-state agent under a statute that makes jurisdictional consequences explicit as a form of consent that remains valid under Pennsylvania Fire and is not displaced by International Shoe, Daimler, and Goodyear, which addressed nonconsenting defendants. Several Justices will view consent as an independent, waivable due-process basis for jurisdiction, and will resist reading Daimler to silently overrule Pennsylvania Fire. The dissenting bloc will argue that this “consent” is coerced and functionally revives the pre-Daimler ‘doing business’ regime, but the majority will likely say that policy concerns are for legislatures and that due process permits jurisdiction by consent. The Court may leave open (or remand on) separate challenges such as Dormant Commerce Clause limits on conditioning market access on all-purpose jurisdiction."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.62,
  "predicted_split": "5-4",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to treat Pennsylvania’s scheme as a form of jurisdiction by consent and to read Pennsylvania Fire as still controlling on the due-process question, distinguishing Daimler and BNSF as limits on non-consensual general jurisdiction. Several Justices are apt to view personal jurisdiction as a waivable individual right and conclude that a corporation can accept general jurisdiction as the price of doing business, leaving any “coercion” or interstate-burden objections to other doctrines (e.g., Dormant Commerce Clause). The narrowest path is to uphold jurisdiction because Pennsylvania law expressly deems registration to be consent, without expanding what counts as being “at home.” A close dissent would argue that this “consent” is not voluntary and functionally nullifies Daimler’s general-jurisdiction limits."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.9,
  "predicted_split": "6-3",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to hold that Title VI incorporates equal-protection limits and that Harvard’s explicit consideration of race in admissions fails strict scrutiny because the asserted “educational benefits of diversity” are not sufficiently measurable or limited, workable race-neutral alternatives exist, and the program lacks a logical stopping point. The current majority has shown strong skepticism toward race-based classifications and is likely to narrow or overrule Grutter/Fisher’s allowance of race-conscious admissions. The Court is also likely to reject Harvard’s standing/mootness defenses and reach the merits, as it has in similar challenges. Even if the Court does not find intentional anti-Asian discrimination on the record, it can still invalidate the program as an impermissible use of race under Title VI."
}

{
  "focal_point": "Whether the Second Circuit’s “right to control” theory—treating deprivation of accurate economic-decision information as “property” loss—can support a federal wire-fraud conviction.",
  "legal_provision": "18 U.S.C. § 1343 (federal wire fraud statute) (and, in the broader related litigation, 18 U.S.C. § 1346 honest-services fraud, though the Ciminelli question presented centers on § 1343).",
  "petitioner_position": "Ciminelli argues that the “right to control” theory impermissibly expands § 1343 beyond its text and traditional understanding by converting an intangible interest in accurate information (or informed decisionmaking) into “property.” He contends the wire-fraud statute targets schemes to obtain money or traditional property, not schemes that merely deprive a victim of potentially valuable information affecting discretionary economic choices. Under his view, allowing liability whenever a defendant withholds or misrepresents information that could influence a transaction turns federal fraud into an all-purpose honesty code, untethered from property as Congress used that term.",
  "respondent_position": "The United States defends the convictions on the ground that the charged scheme involved property fraud within § 1343 because the defendants’ deception targeted the victim’s control over economically valuable assets and decisionmaking in a way that foreseeably exposed the victim to tangible economic harm. On this view, depriving a victim of economically material information can constitute a property deprivation when it affects the victim’s ability to allocate assets or enter transactions on desired terms, and the Second Circuit’s approach fits within wire fraud’s protection of property interests in the context of commercial decisionmaking.",
  "key_precedents": [
    "United States v. Binday, 804 F.3d 558 (2d Cir. 2015)"
  ],
  "legal_complexity": "Complex"
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.9,
  "predicted_split": "9-0",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to hold that § 1343 reaches only schemes to obtain traditional property (money or property) and that an abstract interest in accurate information or “informed economic decisionmaking” is not itself “property.” The Second Circuit’s “right to control” theory effectively collapses materiality and property, turning many deceptive practices into federal felonies even when the victim receives the benefit of the bargain. Recent fraud decisions have repeatedly rejected expansive, judge-made theories that untether federal fraud from concrete property interests. The Court would therefore reverse and require the government to prove a scheme aimed at obtaining money or traditional property, not merely depriving a victim of economically useful information."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.62,
  "predicted_split": "5-4",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to read the statutory term “violation” in § 5321(a)(5)(A) as keyed to the annual reporting obligation under § 5314 (the failure to file a compliant FBAR), not to each account entry on the form, especially given Congress’s explicit account-focused language elsewhere in § 5321(a)(5) (e.g., in the willful-penalty and reasonable-cause provisions). A per-account construction would produce strikingly disproportionate penalties for concededly non-willful conduct, a result the Court often treats as a signal that Congress did not speak clearly enough to authorize it. If any ambiguity remains after text-and-structure analysis, the Court’s skepticism toward expansive civil-penalty interpretations and its tendency to apply lenity-like principles in punitive civil regimes would push toward the per-form cap. The Eighth Amendment issue is unlikely to drive the holding, but the Court may note that the per-form reading avoids serious constitutional concerns."
}

{
  "focal_point": "Whether the Second Circuit’s “right to control” theory—treating deprivation of accurate economic-decision information as a deprivation of “property”—is a valid basis for wire-fraud liability.",
  "legal_provision": "18 U.S.C. § 1343 (federal wire fraud statute) (and, by implication, the meaning of “money or property” in the federal fraud statutes).",
  "petitioner_position": "Ciminelli argues that the “right to control” theory impermissibly expands federal wire fraud beyond its statutory limit to schemes aimed at obtaining “money or property.” In his view, depriving a victim of complete or accurate information relevant to an economic decision is not itself a traditional property interest; it is an “ethereal” interest that effectively turns the wire-fraud statute into an all-purpose prohibition on sharp dealing or nondisclosure. He contends the Second Circuit’s approach allows conviction without proof that the defendant sought to obtain or take identifiable property from the victim, contrary to the text and Supreme Court precedent limiting fraud to traditional property rights.",
  "respondent_position": "The United States defends the convictions on the ground that the charged scheme involved property fraud because it targeted the victim’s economically valuable interests in controlling its assets and making informed allocation decisions. Under the government’s framing, depriving the victim of information necessary to make discretionary economic decisions can constitute a property deprivation when it affects how the victim deploys its money or assets, and the Second Circuit’s doctrine properly captures schemes that manipulate the victim’s decisionmaking to obtain economic benefits. The government thus maintains that the jury instructions and verdict rested on a valid “money or property” theory under § 1343.",
  "key_precedents": [
    "United States v. Binday, 804 F.3d 558 (2d Cir. 2015)"
  ],
  "legal_complexity": "Complex"
}

{
  "focal_point": "Whether 11 U.S.C. § 363(m) strips appellate courts of jurisdiction to review bankruptcy sale/lease orders once a sale is consummated without a stay, or instead merely limits the remedies an appellate court may grant.",
  "legal_provision": "11 U.S.C. § 363(m) (finality protection for good-faith purchasers/lessees in bankruptcy sales/leases under § 363(b) or (c)).",
  "petitioner_position": "MOAC argues that § 363(m) is not jurisdictional because it does not clearly speak in jurisdictional terms; it functions as a nonjurisdictional limitation on the relief an appellate court may award (protecting the validity of a consummated sale/lease to a good-faith purchaser absent a stay), not as a bar to the court’s power to hear the appeal. Therefore, ordinary doctrines like waiver, forfeiture, and judicial estoppel can apply, and the Second Circuit erred by treating § 363(m) as an automatic jurisdictional dismissal rule and by refusing to consider MOAC’s waiver/estoppel arguments.",
  "respondent_position": "Transform (and related respondents) contend that § 363(m) is jurisdictional in operation because it embodies a strong congressional policy of finality for bankruptcy sales/leases and prevents appellate courts from disturbing consummated transactions absent a stay; accordingly, once the transaction closes without a stay, appellate courts lack jurisdiction to grant effective relief and must dismiss. Respondents invoke analogies to jurisdictional time-limit cases (e.g., Bowles) and historical/in rem concepts to argue that Congress intended § 363(m) to operate as a jurisdictional constraint rather than a waivable remedial limitation.",
  "key_precedents": [
    "Bowles v. Russell, 551 U.S. 205 (2007)"
  ],
  "legal_complexity": "Moderate"
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.93,
  "predicted_split": "9-0",
  "reasoning": "The Court is likely to hold that the federal fraud statutes reach only schemes to obtain traditional “money or property,” and that a victim’s interest in receiving accurate information to make better economic decisions is not itself “property” within § 1343. Consistent with McNally, Cleveland, and Kelly, the Justices have repeatedly rejected judge-made expansions that turn fraud into a broad federal integrity or sharp-dealing statute untethered to identifiable property. The “right to control” theory effectively allows conviction without proving the object of the scheme was to obtain the victim’s money or property, so the Court will likely reject it as a valid standalone basis for wire-fraud liability. The convictions would then be vacated or remanded for consideration whether they can be sustained on some other valid property-fraud theory."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
  "p_petitioner_wins": 0.82,
  "predicted_split": "9-0",
  "reasoning": "The Court is very likely to apply its clear-statement rule (e.g., Arbaugh and progeny) and hold that § 363(m) is not jurisdictional because it does not speak in jurisdictional terms and instead presupposes appellate review while limiting the effect of relief on the validity of a consummated sale/lease. The statute functions as a remedial constraint protecting good-faith purchasers absent a stay, not as a limit on the courts’ adjudicatory power, so waiver/forfeiture principles can apply. The Court will probably reject analogies to jurisdictional time limits like Bowles and treat “statutory mootness” here as a merits/remedy issue rather than a jurisdictional bar. It will likely reverse the Second Circuit’s automatic jurisdictional-dismissal approach and remand to consider available relief and any waiver/estoppel arguments."
}

{
  "predicted_winner": "Petitioner",
